{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n6le03ob03re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n6le03ob03re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob03re2img01.jpg", "text": "<strong>Melanie</strong>: If you have spare time, will you explain to me something about data types in JavaScript?<br/>\n<strong>Gordon</strong>: Sure! I'm free right now. JavaScript accepts three types of primitive data: numbers, strings of text and Boolean which has only two states: true and false. Don't forget that JavaScript also defines two trivial data types and each of them holds a single value, those are null and undefined. <br/>\n<strong>Melanie</strong>: Thanks! Ok. Now I'm going to talk about some things I've studied. Will you correct me if I'm mistaken? <br/>\n<strong>Gordon</strong>: Sure!", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob03re2img02.jpg", "text": "<strong>Melanie</strong>: Ok, data can be placed in groups which are named with the keyword \"var\". Those groups are what we call variables. <br/>\n<strong>Gordon</strong>: So far, so good. <br/>\n<strong>Melanie</strong>: Now, the process of storing a value in a variable is called variable initialization which shouldn't be restricted only to the moment in which a variable is created. This one can be initialized later when you need that specific variable. <br/>\nBut, I have a question. If JavaScript is untyped language, does it mean that it isn't necessary to specify the value of data types from the beginning?<br/>\n<strong>Gordon</strong>: Not really. It means that a value of any data type can be held by a JavaScript variable. Therefore, you shouldn't need to indicate the type of value that the variable will be holding during the variable declaration. Now, what could you tell me about the scope of variables?", "time": "39000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob03re2img03.jpg", "text": "<strong>Melanie</strong>: It has to do with the region of the program in which it is defined. JavaScript has only two scopes: global variables which can be defined at anyplace in the JavaScript code; and local variables which will be visible only within the function where they are defined.<br/>\n<strong>Gordon</strong>: And what will happen if you declare a local variable with the same name as a global one?<br/>\n<strong>Melanie</strong>: The global variable is automatically hidden, right?<br/>\n<strong>Gordon</strong>: Indeed. <br/>\n<strong>Melanie</strong>: Thank you very much Gordon! You mustn't be interrupted while grading those exams. I should leave<br/>\n<strong>Gordon</strong>: No problem. You're welcome.", "time": "100500"}]}, "type": "slideshow"}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "type": "WLC", "questions": [], "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "img_path": "", "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can list primitive data types JavaScript supports.  ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can discuss information about JavaScript variables.  ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about both global and local variables.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "What are some important characteristics of JavaScript? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Why are there variables in JavaScript and what do they indicate?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"conten